    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Thanks, I'll try the verbose logging first. The thing is, even when I look at the container logs, everything seems totally fine right up until the crash happens. It's like the environment just... glitches out for no reason. Is there a way to check if it's a resource contention issue, like maybe the Jenkins agent is running out of memory or disk space mid-build, or is that less likely to cause such random dependency errors?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "To investigate resource contention issues, consider breaking down your pipeline to isolate the test stage from Jenkins agents or nodes, and monitor the utilized resources such as memory and disk space during the execution of the test stage:\n\n1. Run the unit tests manually in an isolated environment to see if they fail under similar conditions.\n2. Monitor the build agent's resource metrics as Jenkins jobs run, either by configuring Jenkins' built-in metrics or integrating a separate monitoring tool.\n3. Try increasing the pipeline's timeout settings to account for potential resource exhaustion delays.\n4. If Jenkins is running on a cloud-based infrastructure, review the cloud provider's monitoring tools to identify spikes in resource consumption.\n5. Analyze the Jenkins build logs to discover when and how the resource usage peaks coincide with the errors.\n6. Adjust or optimize the unit tests to reduce memory and disk footprint, possibly redistributing workload if necessary.\n\nResource issues can indeed sometimes manifest in unpredictable ways, particularly in complex environments where multiple factors interact. Consider these steps systematically to shed more light on the problem. Note that Jenkins itself should usually not cause such random errors if properly configured and maintained, but the employment of external services or tools can be a more common culprit in such scenarios.",
      "source": "target",
